            "output": "<path>/home/himmel/projects/open-repo/README.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # open-repo\n2: \n3: A small cross-platform Go replacement for the [`open-repo`](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MandavkarPranjal/environment/refs/heads/main/home/scripts/open-repo) Bash script. It\n4: reads fetch remotes from the current Git repository, lets you choose a remote\n5: and web frontend (including GitHub and GitLab), then opens the resulting\n6: repository URL.\n7: \n8: ## Install\n9: \n10: If Go is installed, install the latest version directly from GitHub:\n11: \n12: ```sh\n13: go install github.com/MandavkarPranjal/open-repo@latest\n14: ```\n15: \n16: This installs `open-repo` into Go's binary directory (`$GOBIN`, or usually\n17: `$HOME/go/bin`). Make sure that directory is on your `PATH`.\n18: \n19: Prebuilt binaries for Linux, macOS, and Windows are available on the\n20: [Releases page](https://github.com/MandavkarPranjal/open-repo/releases).\n21: \n22: ## Build locally\n23: \n24: This project uses [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev/) to pin the Go toolchain used\n25: for development. Install mise, then run this from the repository root:\n26: \n27: ```sh\n28: mise install\n29: ```\n30: \n31: The pinned Go version is `1.26.6`. Available development commands are:\n32: \n33: ```sh\n34: mise run build       # Build the binary\n35: mise run test        # Run all tests\n36: mise run check       # Check formatting, run tests, and run go vet\n37: mise run fmt         # Format Go source files\n38: mise run fmt-check   # Fail if formatting is needed\n39: mise run vet         # Run static analysis\n40: mise run run         # Run open-repo from source\n41: mise run install     # Install open-repo with go install\n42: mise run tidy        # Update go.mod and go.sum\n43: ```\n44: \n45: You can also run the default Go commands directly after `mise install`:\n46: \n47: ```sh\n48: go build -o open-repo .\n49: install -Dm755 open-repo ~/.local/bin/open-repo\n50: ```\n51: \n52: Run `open-repo` from a Git repository. If `fzf` is installed it is used for\n53: both selectors; otherwise the CLI presents numbered choices. Browser opening\n54: uses `omarchy-launch-browser`, `gio`, or `xdg-open` on Linux, `open` on macOS,\n55: and Windows' `rundll32` URL handler. The Zen/uwsm path is preserved on Linux.\n56: \n57: Cross-compile examples:\n58: \n59: ```sh\n60: GOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build -o open-repo .\n61: GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build -o open-repo.exe .\n62: ```\n63: \n64: ## Verify\n65: \n66: ```sh\n67: mise run check\n68: ```\n\n(End of file - total 68 lines)\n</content>",
            "metadata": {
              "preview": "# open-repo\n\nA small cross-platform Go replacement for the [`open-repo`](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MandavkarPranjal/environment/refs/heads/main/home/scripts/open-repo) Bash script. It\nreads fetch remotes from the current Git repository, lets you choose a remote\nand web frontend (including GitHub and GitLab), then opens the resulting\nrepository URL.\n\n## Install\n\nIf Go is installed, install the latest version directly from GitHub:\n\n```sh\ngo install github.com/MandavkarPranjal/open-repo@latest\n```\n\nThis installs `open-repo` into Go's binary directory (`$GOBIN`, or usually\n`$HOME/go/bin`). Make sure that directory is on your `PATH`.\n\nPrebuilt binaries for Linux, macOS, and Windows are available on the\n[Releases page](https://github.com/MandavkarPranjal/open-repo/releases).",
              "truncated": false,
              "loaded": [],
              "display": {
                "type": "file",
                "path": "/home/himmel/projects/open-repo/README.md",
                "text": "# open-repo\n\nA small cross-platform Go replacement for the [`open-repo`](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/MandavkarPranjal/environment/refs/heads/main/home/scripts/open-repo) Bash script. It\nreads fetch remotes from the current Git repository, lets you choose a remote\nand web frontend (including GitHub and GitLab), then opens the resulting\nrepository URL.\n\n## Install\n\nIf Go is installed, install the latest version directly from GitHub:\n\n```sh\ngo install github.com/MandavkarPranjal/open-repo@latest\n```\n\nThis installs `open-repo` into Go's binary directory (`$GOBIN`, or usually\n`$HOME/go/bin`). Make sure that directory is on your `PATH`.\n\nPrebuilt binaries for Linux, macOS, and Windows are available on the\n[Releases page](https://github.com/MandavkarPranjal/open-repo/releases).\n\n## Build locally\n\nThis project uses [mise](https://mise.jdx.dev/) to pin the Go toolchain used\nfor development. Install mise, then run this from the repository root:\n\n```sh\nmise install\n```\n\nThe pinned Go version is `1.26.6`. Available development commands are:\n\n```sh\nmise run build       # Build the binary\nmise run test        # Run all tests\nmise run check       # Check formatting, run tests, and run go vet\nmise run fmt         # Format Go source files\nmise run fmt-check   # Fail if formatting is needed\nmise run vet         # Run static analysis\nmise run run         # Run open-repo from source\nmise run install     # Install open-repo with go install\nmise run tidy        # Update go.mod and go.sum\n```\n\nYou can also run the default Go commands directly after `mise install`:\n\n```sh\ngo build -o open-repo .\ninstall -Dm755 open-repo ~/.local/bin/open-repo\n```\n\nRun `open-repo` from a Git repository. If `fzf` is installed it is used for\nboth selectors; otherwise the CLI presents numbered choices. Browser opening\nuses `omarchy-launch-browser`, `gio`, or `xdg-open` on Linux, `open` on macOS,\nand Windows' `rundll32` URL handler. The Zen/uwsm path is preserved on Linux.\n\nCross-compile examples:\n\n```sh\nGOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build -o open-repo .\nGOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build -o open-repo.exe .\n```\n\n## Verify\n\n```sh\nmise run check\n```",
                "lineStart": 1,
                "lineEnd": 68,
                "totalLines": 68,
                "truncated": false
              }
