        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Now I have a full picture of the project. Here's what would take `open-repo` to the next level:\n\n**Core features:**\n1. **Config file** (`~/.config/open-repo/config.yaml`) — custom frontends, default frontend, per-repo overrides. Currently frontends are hardcoded.\n2. **CLI flags** (`--frontend`, `--remote`, `--file`, `--branch`, `--commit`, `--issue`) — enable non-interactive/scripted usage and open to specific targets (files, PRs, issues).\n3. **Custom/self-hosted frontend templates** — let users define arbitrary `{owner}/{repo}` URL patterns for internal tools.\n\n**Code quality:**\n4. **Expand test coverage** — `gitRemotes`, `chooseRemote`, `tmuxRepositoryDir`, `defaultLinuxBrowser` are untested. Use a `cmdRunner` interface for testable exec mocking.\n5. **Use `embed`** for the frontends map instead of a global var.\n6. **Add `slog`** structured logging + golangci-lint, coverage reporting (`go test -coverprofile`).\n\n**Distribution & DX:**\n7. **Shell completions** (`--generate-shell-completion`) for bash/zsh/fish/powershell.\n8. **Homebrew tap / Scoop / nix** packaging for installers beyond `go install`.\n9. **CONTRIBUTING.md**, `CHANGELOG.md`, and a `CODEOWNERS` file.\n\nThe highest-impact single change would be **config file support with custom frontend templates** — it transforms the tool from a fixed set of providers into a universal repo URL opener.",
